WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:04.590
Dandelions.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.230
Hated by gardeners,

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.100
loved by children.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.780
And also physicists!

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.860
A gentle breeze 
(or tiny pair of human lungs)

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.800
can send these seeds 
cascading into the air

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.960
to disperse,
sometimes for miles.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.600
But until now, how they maintain their flight

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.900
has been a mystery.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.220
Now, scientists studying the fluid dynamics 
of air flow around these seeds

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.800
have discovered a completely new type of flight.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.420
Dandelion seeds float through the air
suspended on a structure called a pappus,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.320
which looks a lot like a parachute.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.580
But unlike a parachute, 
the pappus is actually full of gaps

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.320
with about 90% of the space unfilled

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.980
and this allows air to flow up through the bristles.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.280
Using a laser to illuminate the flow of air,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.300
researchers at the University of Edinburgh 
show that it flows up through the filaments

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.600
in a controlled manner and forms a unique kind of vortex.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.840
The researchers are calling it 
a Separated Vortex Ring

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.200
because it sits away from the pappus.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.000
The air which flows up through the
filaments flows around the vortex

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.140
but, where it makes contact with the edge,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:32.700
the friction causes the air inside the vortex 
to continuously rotate in on itself,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.340
generating a bubble of low pressure.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.340
This vortex bubble sucks 
the dandelion upwards

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.480
as it naturally tries to move 
into the area of low pressure.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.160
This newly uncovered method of flight
 may be common

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.780
in the plant and even animal kingdom,
where bristles abound.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.960
And underwater filter feeders could be using 
this type of fluid dynamics for getting lunch.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.980
Perhaps one day even human 
technologies could be designed

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.400
to fly as efficiently as the mighty dandelion seed

